      content: 'Key areas to assess in suspected fibromyalgia:\n- **Pain distribution**: Ask about pain in all four quadrants of the body plus axial skeleton. Use a body pain diagram to map affected areas.\n- **Associated symptoms**: Non-restorative sleep, fatigue (distinguish from sleepiness), cognitive dysfunction (memory, concentration, word-finding), headaches (tension or migraine), irritable bowel symptoms, interstitial cystitis symptoms, temporomandibular dysfunction.\n- **Symptom modifiers**: Worsened by stress, weather changes, poor sleep, overexertion. Improved by warmth, gentle activity, stress reduction.\n- **Sleep history**: Screen for obstructive sleep apnea (STOP-BANG), restless legs syndrome, and sleep hygiene habits. Alpha-delta sleep intrusion is characteristic.\n- **Psychiatric comorbidities**: Screen for depression (PHQ-9), anxiety (GAD-7), PTSD, and history of childhood adverse experiences (ACE score).\n- **Medication history**: Current and prior analgesics, response to NSAIDs (typically poor in fibromyalgia), any prior trials of pregabalin, duloxetine, or milnacipran.\n- **Functional impact**: Work performance, social participation, exercise habits, ADL limitations.\n- **Family history**: Fibromyalgia has familial aggregation — ask about first-degree relatives with chronic pain, fibromyalgia, or chronic fatigue syndrome.',
