      title: '+ The patient asks: \"Could this all be in my head?\" How do you explain the pathophysiology of fibromyalgia?',
      content: '**Patient-Friendly Explanation**:\n\"Absolutely not — fibromyalgia is a real, physical condition and we understand the biology behind it much better now. Let me explain what is happening in your body.\n\nYour nervous system has a \'volume control\' for pain signals. In fibromyalgia, that volume control is turned up too high. Normal signals from your muscles and joints that should not be painful are being amplified by your brain and spinal cord, making them feel painful. This is called **central sensitization**.\"\n\n**Pathophysiology for the Examiner**:\n1. **Central sensitization**: Augmented central pain processing with increased excitatory neurotransmitters (substance P elevated 3x in CSF, glutamate elevated in insular cortex on MR spectroscopy) and decreased descending inhibitory pathways (reduced serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine).\n\n2. **Functional neuroimaging abnormalities**: fMRI studies demonstrate increased activation in the pain matrix (anterior cingulate cortex, insular cortex, somatosensory cortex) in response to non-painful stimuli.\n\n3. **Small fiber neuropathy**: Emerging evidence shows reduced intraepidermal nerve fiber density on skin biopsy in approximately 40-50% of fibromyalgia patients, providing a peripheral nociceptive driver.\n\n4. **Hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis dysregulation**: Blunted cortisol response to stress, disrupted diurnal cortisol rhythm.\n\n5. **Sleep architecture abnormalities**: Alpha wave intrusion during stage N3 (slow-wave) sleep, reducing restorative sleep quality. This creates a vicious cycle: poor sleep lowers pain thresholds, and pain disrupts sleep.\n\n6. **Neuroinflammation**: PET imaging with TSPO ligands demonstrates activated glial cells in the brain of fibromyalgia patients.\n\n7. **Genetic predisposition**: Polymorphisms in genes encoding COMT (catechol-O-methyltransferase), serotonin transporter (5-HTTLPR), and adrenergic receptors increase vulnerability.',
