    const Section(
      title: '+ Design an evidence-based multimodal treatment program for fibromyalgia, including pharmacologic and non-pharmacologic interventions.',
      content: '**Non-Pharmacologic Interventions (First-Line)**:\n\n1. **Exercise** (strongest evidence base in fibromyalgia):\n- **Aerobic exercise**: Start low, go slow. Begin with 10-15 minutes of low-impact activity (walking, cycling, aquatic exercise) 3x/week and gradually increase to 30-45 minutes, 5x/week. Target 60-70% of age-predicted max heart rate initially.\n- **Aquatic therapy**: Warm-water pool exercise (thermoneutral, 33-34C) is particularly effective due to buoyancy reducing joint stress and warmth reducing pain sensitivity.\n- **Resistance training**: Light weights, 2x/week, emphasizing major muscle groups. Start with 40-50% of 1-repetition max.\n- **Key principle**: Patients will initially feel worse with exercise (post-exertional malaise). Educate that this is expected and will improve with consistency over 4-6 weeks.\n\n2. **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)**: Addresses catastrophizing, fear-avoidance, sleep behaviors, and coping strategies. Recommended weekly sessions for 12-16 weeks. NNT of approximately 4 for clinically meaningful pain reduction.\n\n3. **Sleep Hygiene Optimization**: Fixed sleep/wake times, no screens 1 hour before bed, cool/dark room, limit caffeine after noon. If OSA suspected, polysomnography and CPAP if positive. Treat restless legs if present (check ferritin, consider gabapentin).\n\n4. **Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR)**: 8-week structured program with demonstrated efficacy for fibromyalgia pain and fatigue.\n\n5. **Patient Education**: Pain neuroscience education — explaining the biology of fibromyalgia reduces threat perception and improves self-efficacy.\n\n**Pharmacologic Interventions**:\n\n1. **First-line options** (FDA-approved for fibromyalgia):\n- **Duloxetine 60 mg daily**: SNRI that addresses pain, fatigue, and comorbid depression. Start at 30 mg x 1 week, then increase. Particularly beneficial in this patient given depressive symptoms.\n- **Pregabalin 150-225 mg BID**: Alpha-2-delta calcium channel ligand. Improves pain and sleep. Start at 75 mg at bedtime, titrate over 2-4 weeks. Side effects: weight gain, dizziness, peripheral edema.\n- **Milnacipran 50 mg BID**: SNRI alternative. Better for fatigue-predominant fibromyalgia. Start 12.5 mg daily, titrate over 4 weeks.\n\n2. **Adjunctive agents**:\n- **Low-dose amitriptyline (10-25 mg at bedtime)**: Improves sleep quality and pain. Older but effective. Avoid in elderly or cardiac disease.\n- **Cyclobenzaprine 5-10 mg at bedtime**: Structurally similar to amitriptyline; improves sleep architecture.\n\n3. **Medications to AVOID**:\n- **Opioids**: No evidence of benefit in fibromyalgia and may worsen central sensitization (opioid-induced hyperalgesia). Strongly NOT recommended.\n- **Corticosteroids**: Ineffective and harmful long-term.\n- **Benzodiazepines**: Suppress stage N3 sleep, worsen fatigue, risk of dependence.',
