    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss the challenges of disability documentation in fibromyalgia and your approach to patient advocacy while maintaining objectivity.',
      content: '**Challenges of Disability Documentation in Fibromyalgia**:\n\n1. **Subjective nature of symptoms**: Fibromyalgia lacks objective biomarkers (no lab test, no imaging finding). Disability examiners often discount conditions without objective pathology. This creates a significant barrier for patients.\n\n2. **Inconsistent recognition**: Some insurers and disability systems have historically classified fibromyalgia as a \"subjective\" or \"disputed\" diagnosis, although this has improved since the ACR criteria formalization and growing neuroimaging evidence.\n\n3. **Social Security Disability (SSDI)**: SSA Ruling 12-2p acknowledges fibromyalgia as a medically determinable impairment. It can be established using either 1990 ACR criteria (tender points) or 2010 criteria (WPI + SSS). However, the claimant must still demonstrate that symptoms cause functional limitations precluding substantial gainful activity.\n\n**Approach to Documentation**:\n\n1. **Use validated outcome measures**: Document Fibromyalgia Impact Questionnaire-Revised (FIQR), Widespread Pain Index, Symptom Severity Scale, and functional assessments at every visit. These provide objective tracking over time.\n\n2. **Functional limitations focus**: Document specific functional deficits: \"Patient cannot sustain attention for more than 20 minutes,\" \"Patient requires rest breaks every 45 minutes due to fatigue,\" \"Patient reports inability to stand for more than 30 minutes.\" Avoid vague statements.\n\n3. **Cognitive testing**: Refer for formal neuropsychological testing if cognitive dysfunction is prominent. Objective cognitive data strengthens disability claims significantly.\n\n4. **Treatment adherence documentation**: Show that the patient has engaged in and failed appropriate treatments. Payers are more likely to approve disability if the patient has pursued all reasonable therapeutic options.\n\n5. **Physician advocacy role**: As the treating physiatrist, you can provide a **Medical Source Statement** detailing the patient\'s residual functional capacity (RFC). Be honest and evidence-based — do not exaggerate or minimize. Your credibility as an expert is your most valuable tool for the patient.\n\n6. **Consider partial disability or accommodations first**: Before full disability, explore FMLA leave, reduced work hours, ergonomic modifications, cognitive accommodations (written instructions, reduced multitasking), and a gradual return-to-work plan. Full disability should be a last resort as it often worsens outcomes in fibromyalgia (deconditioning, social isolation, loss of purpose).',
